<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sir William Johnson, 1st Baronet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1715">1715</a>–<a href="/years/1774">1774</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Sir William Johnson</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir William Johnson was an Irish-born colonial official in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> who became British superintendent of Indian affairs for the northern colonies in 1756. Settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> valley, he learned the Mohawk language, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molly-brant">Molly Brant</a> as his consort and built close ties with the Iroquois. He led colonial and Iroquois forces to victory at Lake George in 1755, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-niagara">Fort Niagara</a> in 1759 and negotiated the Treaty of Fort Stanwix in 1768.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-johnson.jpg" alt="Sir William Johnson, 1st Baronet" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_William_Johnson_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their councils, oratory and wampum diplomacy set the forms of treaty-making that colonial officials adopted, and <b>Sir William Johnson</b>, British superintendent of Indian Affairs, built his influence on close ties with the Mohawk.</blockquote><a href="/empire/iroquois-confederacy">Iroquois Confederacy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 18th century Hendrick Theyanoguin, Molly Brant and Joseph Brant built close ties with Britain and the Indian superintendent <b>William Johnson</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/mohawk-nation">Mohawk Nation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in County Meath, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. Died in the Province of New York. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.67,-6.67&amp;year=1753" aria-label="County Meath on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4402 320 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sir William Johnson, 1st Baronet"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4852 590h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4852 590h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.67,-6.67&amp;year=1753">What was here in 1753? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
